\section{Chapter 7: HYDRA and TARTAN: Distributed Modular Intelligence}
&nbsp;
\subsection{7.1 Introduction: From Centralized to Distributed Cognition}
&nbsp;
The previous chapters formalized RSVP‚Äôs foundations in field theory (Part I) and its
applications to semantic infrastructure, agency, and economics (Part II). We now turn
to the architectures that operationalize these principles in practice:
\textbf{HYDRA} (Hierarchical, Yet Distributed Relational Architecture) and
\textbf{TARTAN} (Tensorial Agency for Recursive Trajectory Alignment in Networks).
Together, these systems instantiate RSVP‚Äôs principles in multi-agent, distributed
contexts, enabling modular intelligence that scales without centralization.
&nbsp;
HYDRA models local RSVP dynamics across nodes, while TARTAN ensures global coherence
through functorial coordination. These architectures resist monopolistic attractors
by distributing agency and preserving entropic sovereignty across networks of
semantic modules.
&nbsp;
